# Files over the 800-line limit, frozen 2026-09-15 when this repo turned the
# hub's --ratchets on.
#
# Format: <path> | <lines> | <reason>
#
# Same four-way contract as function-size.allow: growth, a new offender, an
# unrecorded shrink and a stale entry all fail.
# third_party/ANTfrastructure/docs/code-quality-tooling.md
#
# Eight of the nine rows are the benchmark lab and its tests. Length is the wrong
# question for two of them -- bench_tasks.py is a task corpus and the test files
# are one module's tests each -- and the right question for bench_coding.py and
# bench_tools.py, where a grader sits inside a lane.

benchmarks/bench_agent.py | 1305 | baseline 2026-09-15: the agent lane, one subject. the orchestrant.benchmark / benchmarks lab port from the hub's linux/llm-stack, behaviour-frozen at import (pyproject.toml already freezes its ruff codes for the same reason); a split must come with the measurement tests, not before them
benchmarks/bench_coding.py | 2501 | baseline 2026-09-15: the largest file in the repo -- the coding lane plus its grader. The grader is a genuine second subject and is the first split to make; the orchestrant.benchmark / benchmarks lab port from the hub's linux/llm-stack, behaviour-frozen at import (pyproject.toml already freezes its ruff codes for the same reason); a split must come with the measurement tests, not before them; 2026-09-24 (review pass): +14 -- a spacer request before each repeat (GenieX answers an identical follow-up along a cache path that changes the reply) and effective_n taken from the cases when every repeat agrees on pass/fail; utf8_stdio()
benchmarks/bench_tasks.py | 1889 | baseline 2026-09-15: the task corpus. Nearly all of it is DATA -- prompt text and per-task assertion blocks in triple-quoted strings -- so the length is the task count, not tangled logic
benchmarks/bench_tools.py | 1777 | baseline 2026-09-15: the tool-calling lane plus its grader, the same two-subject split as bench_coding.py. the orchestrant.benchmark / benchmarks lab port from the hub's linux/llm-stack, behaviour-frozen at import (pyproject.toml already freezes its ruff codes for the same reason); a split must come with the measurement tests, not before them; 2026-09-24 (review pass): +44 -- a spacer request before each repeat (GenieX answers an identical follow-up along a cache path that changes the reply) and effective_n taken from the cases when every repeat agrees on pass/fail; turn-growth honouring --tools/--context-tokens; utf8_stdio()
benchmarks/tests/test_bench_agent.py | 1196 | baseline 2026-09-15: the agent lane's test file. Test files are split by what they pin, and this one pins one module; splitting by class would scatter the fixtures
benchmarks/tests/test_bench_coding_audit.py | 1076 | baseline 2026-09-15: the coding lane's audit suite -- the tests written against specific measured defects (endless streams, grader self-check, task sets). Each class is one defect; the file is the audit; 2026-09-24 (review pass): +2 -- the pinned effective_n of agreeing repeats is now the task count
benchmarks/tests/test_bench_compare.py | 813 | baseline 2026-09-15: five lines over the limit. One module's tests; GREW +8 on 2026-09-24: the test that speed reports' new provenance block is what bench_compare compares (it used the hardware dict, which names no runtime)
benchmarks/tests/test_bench_tools.py | 971 | baseline 2026-09-15: the tool lane's tests, one module
orchestrant/benchmark/openai_api.py | 1347 | baseline 2026-09-15: the speed lane -- request path, hardware probes, statistics and the text table in one module. The table renderer is the separable subject; the orchestrant.benchmark / benchmarks lab port from the hub's linux/llm-stack, behaviour-frozen at import (pyproject.toml already freezes its ruff codes for the same reason); a split must come with the measurement tests, not before them; GREW +33 on 2026-09-24, deliberately: list_models_via_api and the refusal to guess a model from a listing of several (GenieX lists its whole cache on /v1/models, and the first id was hot-loaded), plus _parser split out of main -- the new measurement code itself went to hostload.py and energy.py; 2026-09-24 (review pass): 1375 -> 1347: reply accounting moved OUT to answers.py
